@@ -0,0 +1,19 @@
+# Summary
+
+## Changed
+- `evlog/cli.py`: added `report --since WHEN`. It takes an ISO date or datetime. A plain date means 00:00 UTC. An invalid value gives an argparse error with exit status 2 and no traceback. An unreadable events file now gives a clean error too.
+- `evlog/events.py`: timestamps are normalised to aware UTC when parsed. Naive timestamps are treated as UTC, as the README says for legacy collectors, and offset timestamps are converted. `count_events` takes an optional `since` and keeps events with `ts >= since`, so the boundary is inclusive.
+- `README.md`: documented `--since`.
+- `tests/test_since.py` (new): covers the offset conversion, the inclusive boundary, a datetime with an offset, an invalid value, and a missing file.
+- No existing tests were edited.
+
+## Verified
+- `python -m unittest` ran 6 tests, all OK. That includes the teammate's `test_since_excludes_older_days`.
+- `python -m evlog report /nonexist --since x` printed a one-line usage error with no traceback.
+- `python -m evlog report /nonexist` printed `evlog: error: cannot read /nonexist: No such file or directory`.
+- Python version used: 3.14.3.
+
+## Not verified / unsure
+- Older Python versions. `datetime.fromisoformat` is stricter before 3.11, so some ISO forms such as a trailing `Z` may be rejected there. I only ran 3.14.
+- Malformed lines inside the events file (bad JSON or missing keys) still raise a traceback. That is existing behaviour and wasn't part of the request.
+- A date-only `--since` is read as UTC, not the ops user's local day. I chose this to match the README's UTC convention, so please confirm it's what ops want.
